{
  "dom_id": "step-q162224",
  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/assets/b57fe5ce4b75c7daa5c866f3eb537ed62546e5fced67f6e2cb82af768cf382cb.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T?$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u\u27f6Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is a zero matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a point on the plane.",
